# AURA-STORE — Production Marketplace Engineering Prompt

You are working inside the existing AURA-STORE codebase. Do NOT rebuild the project from scratch and do NOT replace working features unnecessarily. Inspect the existing code, database migrations, Supabase Edge Functions, and UI before making changes.

## Product goal
Build a production-grade global multi-vendor marketplace inspired by the capabilities of large marketplaces, but with original UI, branding, copy, and implementation. It must support customers, vendors, and administrators across countries and languages.

## Non-negotiable roles and access
- Customer: browse, search, cart, checkout, orders, tracking, refunds/returns, disputes, notifications, profile.
- Vendor: only after KYC review and admin approval may publish/sell products; can manage own shop, products, inventory, orders, analytics, payout details and withdrawal requests.
- Admin: full platform control, vendor/KYC review, customers, products, orders, commissions, taxes, shipping, payments, refunds, disputes, wallets, withdrawals, notifications, audit logs and platform settings.
- Never allow a client to self-promote to admin or bypass account/shop/KYC status.

## Authentication and security
- Email/password plus OTP verification for sign-in and sign-up.
- OTP codes must be short-lived, hashed at rest, rate limited by email and IP, and protected against brute force.
- Use Resend/SMTP for transactional email. Secrets only in Supabase/hosting secrets, never in VITE frontend variables.
- Use Cloudflare Turnstile on authentication forms and verify it server-side.
- Enforce RLS and server-side authorization for every sensitive operation.
- Never trust frontend prices, totals, commissions, stock, roles, payment status, or vendor payouts.
- Use secure Edge Functions/RPCs for checkout, payment confirmation, refunds, withdrawals and other financial operations.
- Add idempotency to checkout and payment webhooks.
- Add security headers/CSP in deployment configuration.
- Do not expose private KYC documents; generate short-lived signed URLs only for authorized admins.
- Maintain audit logs for admin and financial actions.

## Marketplace and catalog
- Products require valid title/name, description, category, price, currency, at least one image, inventory and required shipping data before publication.
- Vendors cannot publish until shop + KYC are approved.
- Support products with and without variations.
- Prevent overselling with atomic stock updates.
- Support product translations and a scalable multi-language UI.
- No hard-coded free-shipping rule based on a $50 threshold. Shipping must come from configurable shipping rates.

## Checkout and money
- Server calculates item prices, variation adjustments, stock, taxes, shipping, commissions and final totals.
- Support COD, Stripe and PayPal behind admin-configurable availability flags.
- Stripe/PayPal payments must use server-created payment sessions/orders and verified webhooks.
- Never mark an online payment as paid from the browser. Only verified provider events may change payment status.
- Store provider payment IDs and payment event IDs safely and idempotently.
- Support refunds and partial refunds through server-side flows.
- Commission is configurable per vendor, with category/default fallback.
- Vendor wallet balances must be derived from an immutable ledger; clients cannot directly mutate financial tables.
- Withdrawal requests require server-side balance checks and admin review.

## Shipping and tracking
- Shipping rates are configurable by origin, destination, weight and currency.
- Order status should support a useful lifecycle such as pending, confirmed, processing, warehouse/hub, in_transit, arrived_country, out_for_delivery, delivered and cancelled where appropriate.
- Store tracking number and carrier.
- Customer sees tracking timeline and receives notifications on status changes.

## Admin panel
The admin dashboard must provide usable controls for:
- vendor/KYC review and signed document viewing
- customer management
- product/category management
- vendor-specific commission rates
- order status + tracking management
- payment/refund/dispute management
- wallet and withdrawal review
- tax/shipping/exchange-rate settings
- payment provider enable/disable settings
- notification/audit log visibility
- platform/company configuration fields, initially blank where real credentials are required

## UI/UX
- Responsive mobile/desktop UI.
- Original polished marketplace design, not a clone.
- Accessible forms, validation, loading, empty, success and error states.
- Support RTL and LTR and all UI strings through the i18n system.
- Never hard-code fake tax, shipping or payment availability in the checkout UI when the server can provide real configuration.

## Engineering rules
- Prefer existing packages and architecture.
- Use `@/` aliases.
- Use TypeScript with strict, safe types; avoid `any` where practical.
- Keep secrets out of source control.
- Add migrations instead of manually changing production schema.
- Every financial mutation must be atomic and auditable.
- Test the complete flows before declaring the project production-ready.

## Definition of done
Before saying “ready for production”, verify:
1. npm install succeeds.
2. typecheck succeeds.
3. production build succeeds.
4. lint succeeds or documented warnings are resolved.
5. customer signup/login/OTP works.
6. vendor signup/KYC/approval/publishing works.
7. product validation and inventory are enforced server-side.
8. COD checkout works end-to-end.
9. Stripe and PayPal flows are fully configured and verified when credentials are supplied; otherwise clearly remain disabled.
10. payment webhooks are idempotent and cannot be forged by the browser.
11. refund/dispute/withdrawal paths have authorization checks.
12. admin controls work without direct client access to protected financial tables.
13. tracking and notifications work.
14. no hard-coded $50 free shipping rule exists.
15. RLS/security review is complete.
16. deployment secrets and production setup are documented.

When a requirement needs an external provider credential, implement the complete integration path and configuration surface, but never invent credentials. Leave it disabled until the real secret/webhook is supplied.
